WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:10.226
Ten days passed, and there was no news of Mr. Rochester. Mrs. Fairfax said she wouldn't be surprised if he went abroad for a year.

2
00:00:10.227 --> 00:00:15.019
He'd often left Thornfield in the past in an abrupt manner.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.625
When I heard this, I actually allowed myself to feel disappointed.

4
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:27.888
But then I remembered that I had nothing to do with the master of Thornfield except to receive his salary for teaching Adele.

5
00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:31.774
I should not waste my love on someone who was above me.

6
00:00:31.774 --> 00:00:37.858
Mr. Rochester had been absent for more than two weeks when Mrs. Fairfax received a letter.

7
00:00:37.858 --> 00:00:45.685
"Now I suppose we'll know whether we're to expect his return or not," she said as she opened his letter at breakfast.

8
00:00:45.685 --> 00:00:51.343
My hand shook, and I spilled half my coffee as she read the letter.

9
00:00:51.344 --> 00:01:01.081
I tied Adele's pinafore, which happened to be loose, and then helped her to another bun and refilled her milk before I trusted myself to speak.

10
00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:07.079
"Mr. Rochester isn't likely to return soon?" I asked in a nonchalant voice.

11
00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:18.249
"Indeed he's returning in three days and he's bringing some houseguests. He has given me instructions to hire some help from Millcote to prepare the house."

12
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:30.811
The next few days were busy. Mrs. Fairfax needed me in the kitchen, so I spent all my time helping—or hindering—the cook as she made pastries and prepared game.

13
00:01:30.812 --> 00:01:37.650
Since I was busy, Adele was excused from school and she ran wild with excitement.

14
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:47.892
During these days the one blot on my happiness was Grace Poole. The dinner hour was the only time she spent with her fellow servants.

15
00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:51.494
But none of them seemed to think this was strange.

16
00:01:51.494 --> 00:02:01.883
One day I overheard one of the new servants from Millcote talking to Leah about Grace, but as soon as Leah spotted me, they stopped.

17
00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:05.300
"Doesn't she know?" whispered the woman.

18
00:02:05.301 --> 00:02:13.205
Leah shook her head, and I was reminded that there was a mystery at Thornfield and that I was excluded from it.

19
00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:20.855
At last that evening we heard the clatter of hoofbeats that signaled the arrival of Mr. Rochester and his party.

20
00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:33.837
Looking out a window, I saw Mr. Rochester and a dark-haired woman, who had to be Blanche Ingram, galloping on horseback ahead of two carriages full of ladies and gentlemen.

21
00:02:33.838 --> 00:02:44.593
After the ladies had changed, Adele longed to go downstairs and see their beautiful dresses, but we had our supper upstairs in the schoolroom.

22
00:02:44.594 --> 00:02:54.106
I told her stories for as long as she would listen. Then we sat on the stairs as the sound of a piano floated up from the sitting room.

23
00:02:54.107 --> 00:03:01.405
A woman sang, and then we heard a duet. I listened for Mr. Rochester's voice.

24
00:03:01.406 --> 00:03:09.987
The next day Mrs. Fairfax said she'd mentioned to Mr. Rochester that Adele wanted to be introduced to the ladies.

25
00:03:09.987 --> 00:03:14.818
He'd said that Adele and I should come to the sitting room after dinner.

26
00:03:14.818 --> 00:03:25.631
And so, wearing my best gray dress, the one I'd worn only to Miss Temple's wedding, I settled myself unobtrusively on a window seat.

27
00:03:25.632 --> 00:03:41.130
Suddenly six ladies entered the room. Mrs. Eshton had two pretty daughters, Amy and Louisa, but they could not compete with Lady Ingram and her tall, beautiful daughters, Mary and Blanche.

28
00:03:41.131 --> 00:03:48.704
No longer able to contain her excitement, Adele ran over and greeted them in French.

29
00:03:48.704 --> 00:03:55.886
Blanche looked down at her with disdain, but the Eshton girls exclaimed with delight over Adele.

30
00:03:55.886 --> 00:04:01.191
They led her to a sofa, where she chattered away in French and English.

31
00:04:01.192 --> 00:04:12.035
At last coffee was served, and the gentlemen entered the room: Mr. Rochester, Mr. Eshton, and Theodore Ingram, brother of Mary and Blanche.

32
00:04:12.036 --> 00:04:22.365
Mr. Rochester didn't even glance my way, so I was free to watch him. Reader, you know I did not intend to love him.

33
00:04:22.365 --> 00:04:31.328
In fact, I had worked hard at pulling the roots of love from my soul. But he made me love him without looking at me.

34
00:04:31.328 --> 00:04:36.986
I knew I must conceal my feelings and remember that he couldn't care for me.

35
00:04:36.986 --> 00:04:49.830
Mr. Rochester had not noticed me, but Blanche had. "Why didn't you send Adele to school?" she asked Mr. Rochester. "A governess is such a nuisance."

36
00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:57.903
"I couldn't afford it," replied Mr. Rochester, still not looking at me. "Schools are expensive."

37
00:04:57.904 --> 00:05:06.459
"Theodore and I used to delight in tormenting our governesses with tricks. Do you remember those merry days, Theodore?"

38
00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.624
"Indeed I do," replied her brother with a laugh.

39
00:05:10.625 --> 00:05:21.705
Blanche now seated herself with proud grace at the piano. Spreading out her dress like a queen, she played a brilliant prelude, talking all the time.

40
00:05:21.706 --> 00:05:28.968
Both her words and her manner seemed intended to excite the admiration and amazement of her listeners.

41
00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:40.187
"Oh, I'm sick of today's young men!" she exclaimed. "They can't do anything without their mama's permission, and they're so absorbed in their own pretty faces.

42
00:05:40.187 --> 00:05:46.861
When I marry, I'll refuse to share my husband with the image he sees in his mirror."

43
00:05:46.862 --> 00:05:55.669
She paused, but no one said anything. "Now sing, Mr. Rochester," she commanded, "and I will play for you."

44
00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:04.910
"I am all obedience" was his response, and he began to sing in a powerful bass voice that quickly found its way into my heart.

45
00:06:04.911 --> 00:06:11.427
Now was my time to slip away, but I found I could not leave until Mr. Rochester finished.

46
00:06:11.428 --> 00:06:21.904
When the song ended and conversation began, I left the room. Crossing the hall, I noticed that my shoe was loose, so I bent to tie it.

47
00:06:21.904 --> 00:06:30.572
I heard a door open and a man come into the hall. Rising hastily, I came face to face with Mr. Rochester.

48
00:06:30.573 --> 00:06:33.129
"How do you do?" he asked.

49
00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.070
"I'm very well, sir."

50
00:06:35.071 --> 00:06:38.043
"Why didn't you come over and speak to me?"

51
00:06:38.044 --> 00:06:43.062
"I didn't want to disturb you as you seemed engaged, sir."

52
00:06:43.063 --> 00:06:46.135
"What have you been doing during my absence?"

53
00:06:46.136 --> 00:06:48.957
"Only teaching Adele as usual."

54
00:06:48.957 --> 00:06:53.824
"And getting a good deal paler than you were. What's the matter?"

55
00:06:53.825 --> 00:06:57.205
"Nothing at all, sir. I'm just tired."

56
00:06:57.205 --> 00:07:02.063
"And a little depressed too," he said. "Tell me what's wrong."

57
00:07:02.064 --> 00:07:06.713
"Nothing . . . nothing, sir. I'm not depressed."

58
00:07:06.714 --> 00:07:15.023
"But you are depressed. A few more words and you would be crying. There, I see a tear in your eye even now."

59
00:07:15.024 --> 00:07:18.710
He looked at me with some concern and tenderness.

60
00:07:18.711 --> 00:07:29.434
"Well, I'll excuse you for leaving early tonight, but as long as my visitors stay, I expect you to appear in the sitting room every evening. Now goodnight, my . . ."

61
00:07:29.435 --> 00:07:34.516
He stopped, bit his lip, and abruptly left me.

